Subject: System Settings: the window's and KPageView's page stacks are reported as plain containers, not unnamed layered panes

Two stacked widgets hold System Settings' content one page at a time,
and neither has a name: the window's page stack, the QStackedWidget
SettingsBase sets as its central widget, and KPageView's page stack,
the KPageStackedWidget that holds the open settings page. Both take
Qt's default role for a stacked widget, a layered pane. Orca speaks the
window's stack as "layered pane 0 items" after the window title every
time a System Settings window opens, and KPageView's stack the same way
before the first control on every Tab or Shift+Tab into a settings page.
As plain containers (QAccessible::Client, AT-SPI filler) both are passed
over, like the scroll area System Settings names "Scrollable area"
inside them.

An accessibility factory installed before the main window is built
answers for KPageStackedWidget, and for a QStackedWidget only when its
parent is SettingsBase, which the stack's constructor sets before any
accessible query. Installed factories are asked before Qt's plugins at
each level of the class chain, so every other stacked widget, in any
module, keeps its role, and each stack's children are still its pages.

--- a/app/main.cpp
+++ b/app/main.cpp
@@ -17,11 +17,33 @@
 #include <KLocalizedString>
 #include <KPluginMetaData>
 #include <KWindowSystem>
+#include <QAccessible>
+#include <QAccessibleWidget>
 #include <iostream>
 
 #include "SettingsBase.h"
 #include "kcmmetadatahelpers.h"
 
+// Two stacks hold the window's content one page at a time and have no name
+// of their own: the window's page stack, the QStackedWidget SettingsBase sets
+// as its central widget, and KPageView's page stack inside it. Reported as a
+// layered pane, a screen reader speaks either as an unnamed ancestor, after
+// the window title when the window opens and on every move into a settings
+// page; reported as a plain container it is passed over, like the
+// "Scrollable area" each page sits in. The window's stack is told apart by
+// its parent, so every other QStackedWidget in a module keeps its role.
+static QAccessibleInterface *pageStackAccessibleFactory(const QString &classname, QObject *object)
+{
+    if (!object || !object->isWidgetType()) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    if (classname == QLatin1String("KPageStackedWidget")
+        || (classname == QLatin1String("QStackedWidget") && object->parent() && object->parent()->inherits("SettingsBase"))) {
+        return new QAccessibleWidget(static_cast<QWidget *>(object), QAccessible::Client);
+    }
+    return nullptr;
+}
+
 int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 {
